<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Corinna</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Corinna of Tanagra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Corinna was a lyric poet from Tanagra in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> who wrote narrative poems on local myths in the Boeotian dialect. Ancient tradition made her a contemporary and rival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a>, said to have beaten him in competition and advised him on the use of myth, but many modern scholars date her to the third century BCE. Fragments of her work survive on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/corinna.jpg" alt="Corinna" width="320" height="492" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frederic Leighton · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leighton_corinna.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Corinna came from Tanagra in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>. The Suda, a tenth-century encyclopedia, records her as the daughter of Acheloodorus and Procratia and gives her the nickname Myia, &quot;the fly&quot;. Ancient tradition placed her in the fifth century BCE as a contemporary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a>, either his teacher or a fellow pupil of Myrtis of Anthedon, and said she defeated him in at least one poetry competition, though some sources claim five victories.</p><p>Scholars have been divided over this chronology since Edgar Lobel argued in 1930 that there is no reason to think she predates the spelling used on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> papyrus. Sceptics note that no ancient writer mentions her before the first century BCE; Martin Litchfield West and David A. Campbell favour a late date, and Campbell calls the third century &quot;almost certain&quot;. Archibald Allen and Jiří Frel defend the traditional date.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>According to the Suda she wrote five books of poetry, collected in a Boeotian edition in the late third or early second century BCE. About forty fragments survive, more than for any ancient woman poet except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sappho">Sappho</a>. The three substantial ones come from second-century CE papyri found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. One tells of a singing contest between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-cithaeron">Mount Cithaeron</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-helicon">Mount Helicon</a>, ending with Helicon hurling down a boulder in anger. Another tells of the daughters of the river-god Asopus, abducted by gods, and a third invokes Terpsichore, the Muse of dance and chorus.</p><p>She wrote in a literary dialect that mixed her Boeotian speech with the language of epic, and her style is clear, simple and often humorous. Her subjects are local legends, including Orion, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oedipus">Oedipus</a> and the Seven against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, which she frequently reworks with details found nowhere else. In a story told by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, she rebuked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a> for neglecting myth, and after he filled his next ode with allusions she advised him, &quot;Sow with the hand, not with the sack.&quot;</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Pausanias reports a monument to her in the streets of Tanagra and a painting of her in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a>, and the theologian Tatian says the sculptor Silanion made her portrait statue. Her poetry was popular in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> counted her among nine mortal muses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a> may have borrowed her name for his lover in the Amores, and wall-paintings at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompeii">Pompeii</a> apparently show her victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a>.</p><p>Modern critics have often judged her parochial and dull, while others now see her reworking Panhellenic traditions to give Boeotian figures a larger role. She has also drawn the interest of feminist literary historians as one of the few ancient Greek women poets whose work survives.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Corinna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pindar of Thebes was the greatest choral lyric poet of the fifth century, <b>Corinna of Tanagra</b> was remembered as his rival, and the much earlier poet Hesiod had lived at Ascra below Mount Helicon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Tanagra was the home of the lyric poet <b>Corinna</b>, said to have competed with Pindar.</blockquote><a href="/state/tanagra">Tanagra</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tanagra, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.33,23.54" aria-label="Tanagra on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Corinna"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4567 4278h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4567 4278h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.33,23.54">What was here? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
